1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,020
A zipper has no lock,
no hook, and no glue.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,590
And yet it holds.

3
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:08,050
The secret is hidden
inside this tiny slider.

4
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:12,160
In four minutes: two rows of
teeth that never line up...

5
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:14,850
...and one tiny Y that makes them lock.

6
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:16,590
Let's start with the teeth.

7
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:22,260
Open a zipper: two tapes, each
edged with a row of teeth.

8
00:00:23,042 --> 00:00:27,992
Look closely. The two rows don't
match: one is shifted by half a tooth.

9
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:31,552
So every tooth on one side
faces a gap on the other.

10
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:36,782
If the teeth lined up, they would hit
head-on. Shifted, each one slips in between

11
00:00:36,802 --> 00:00:37,852
two.

12
00:00:37,902 --> 00:00:41,712
Closed, a common chain is only
about five millimetres wide.

13
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:46,282
Every tooth sits between two neighbours.
But that alone holds nothing.

14
00:00:47,662 --> 00:00:49,892
So what does one tooth actually look like?

15
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:53,812
On top, a small bump.
Underneath, a hollow.

16
00:00:54,342 --> 00:00:59,752
The bump of one tooth drops into the
hollow of the next, like nested spoons.

17
00:01:00,282 --> 00:01:04,462
And the head of each tooth is wider
than the gap it sits in. Pull the tapes

18
00:01:04,482 --> 00:01:06,652
sideways, and nothing slides out.

19
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:11,812
But that same shape causes a problem.
Push two rows straight together...

20
00:01:12,092 --> 00:01:15,802
...and they jam. The wide heads
can't get past each other.

21
00:01:16,262 --> 00:01:18,972
So how does a slider do
what your fingers can't?

22
00:01:19,242 --> 00:01:20,532
Let's look inside one.

23
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:26,247
Make the slider transparent, and
you find a channel shaped like a Y.

24
00:01:26,707 --> 00:01:31,537
Two arms in at the top, one way out at
the bottom, and a divider in between.

25
00:01:32,416 --> 00:01:34,966
Each row of teeth runs down one arm.

26
00:01:35,426 --> 00:01:40,816
As the arms close in, the channel bends the
chain very slightly, and the teeth tilt.

27
00:01:41,336 --> 00:01:45,756
That tilt is the whole trick. A tilted
tooth clears the head of the one ahead of

28
00:01:45,776 --> 00:01:46,456
it...

29
00:01:46,476 --> 00:01:50,366
...then it straightens, and its
bump drops into the hollow. Click.

30
00:01:50,866 --> 00:01:55,396
Then the next one. And the next.
Again and again, all the way up.

31
00:01:55,706 --> 00:02:00,076
The walls of the Y do the pushing,
at the same angle, every time.

32
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:04,086
Your fingers can't do that.
The slider does it for you.

33
00:02:05,577 --> 00:02:07,547
So what happens when you pull it down?

34
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:12,677
That divider in the middle is a wedge. It
pushes between the teeth and pries them

35
00:02:12,697 --> 00:02:13,737
apart.

36
00:02:13,757 --> 00:02:18,597
And because the wedge has to stay between
the last two teeth, no zipper is ever

37
00:02:18,617 --> 00:02:19,827
completely closed.

38
00:02:20,717 --> 00:02:23,987
Stops at both ends keep the
slider from running off the chain.

39
00:02:24,507 --> 00:02:29,827
And the pull tab is more than a handle. On
many sliders, when it lies flat, a small

40
00:02:29,847 --> 00:02:32,977
pin drops between the
teeth and holds the slider.

41
00:02:34,016 --> 00:02:37,446
Up, the Y locks the teeth.
Down, the wedge opens them.

42
00:02:39,146 --> 00:02:41,276
Not every zipper has
separate teeth, though.

43
00:02:41,706 --> 00:02:47,146
Coil zippers, the most common kind, use a
plastic thread wound into a spiral, one on

44
00:02:47,166 --> 00:02:48,216
each tape.

45
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:52,922
Molded zippers have plastic teeth
formed right onto the edge of the tape.

46
00:02:53,292 --> 00:02:58,392
And metal teeth are cut from wire, stamped
with their bump and hollow, and clamped

47
00:02:58,412 --> 00:02:59,862
on, one by one.

48
00:03:00,698 --> 00:03:03,188
Three ways to make teeth. One slider.

49
00:03:03,748 --> 00:03:09,158
In eighteen ninety-three, a Chicago inventor
patented a shoe fastener made of hooks.

50
00:03:09,718 --> 00:03:13,718
Twenty years later, an engineer swapped
the hooks for teeth that nest into each

51
00:03:13,738 --> 00:03:16,548
other. The patent came
in nineteen seventeen.

52
00:03:17,218 --> 00:03:21,708
The name came in nineteen twenty-three,
from rubber boots and the sound they made.

53
00:03:22,258 --> 00:03:23,308
Zip.

54
00:03:24,516 --> 00:03:26,026
So why do zippers fail?

55
00:03:26,781 --> 00:03:28,291
Lose a single tooth...

56
00:03:28,571 --> 00:03:33,641
...and its neighbours have nothing left to
hold. The chain opens, tooth after tooth.

57
00:03:34,472 --> 00:03:39,312
More often, it's the slider. With wear,
its mouth spreads a little, and the rows

58
00:03:39,332 --> 00:03:40,842
slip apart behind it.

59
00:03:41,142 --> 00:03:46,352
The fix: squeeze the slider very gently
with pliers, until the teeth lock again.

60
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:52,410
Stuck instead? Rub a pencil on the
teeth. Graphite is a dry lubricant.

61
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,750
Two rows, half a tooth apart.

62
00:03:56,654 --> 00:03:58,644
One tiny Y. And it holds.

63
00:04:00,442 --> 00:04:01,982
Subscribe for a new one every week.
